The window's words were all in the catalogs (1339); what broke it was the page's translation of the dialog's message, one text under white-space: pre-line: norm() made it one line and a one-line pattern ("GPU{#0} · {1}", "{0}: {1}") matched across the whole, so the lines came back joined. A text whose line breaks show is now looked up whole by a key that holds it (a key of its own lines, or a pattern whose key had them), else a line at a time; only when no line of it is known does the whole go to the patterns as before, and it is taken only where it says something new. A text whose breaks do not show (the page source's own wrapping) is read across as before. The element's style is asked only for a text of several lines the catalog does not know whole, and in the walk all of them at once: the page has four such texts the walker reaches, all known whole, so a language's switch reads no style (the first try, a style read a text, had made the switch to Russian 1.9 s from 1.2; now as before, measured twice on each page). The scan probed in all seven languages: 7, 8 and 7 lines in each, as in English; its toasts translated. Suite 684/0 (suite-1359.part: the scan in Russian line by line; a key of its own lines whole; a soft line break read across by its pattern; lines each a key; lines in the language already left on their lines).

